1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,144
They locked me away for a murder I never committed, leaving my oil portrait in a dusty attic.

2
00:00:06,444 --> 00:00:13,740
Ten long years crawled by in absolute darkness, but my face in the mirror refused to wrinkle.

3
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:20,592
Today, my parole officer brought me home and handed me the heavy, gold-framed canvas from the attic.

4
00:00:20,892 --> 00:00:26,052
I wiped away a decade of dust, expecting to see the young man I used to be.

5
00:00:26,352 --> 00:00:33,168
Instead, the painted eyes stared back at me sunken, bruised, and thoroughly ravaged by brutal time.

6
00:00:33,468 --> 00:00:40,212
Deep wrinkles cut across the canvas forehead where my skin remains completely smooth and unblemished today.

7
00:00:40,512 --> 00:00:48,144
The frame grew heavy in my trembling hands as I realized the terrible truth of my sentence.

8
00:00:48,444 --> 00:00:55,332
The prison didn't hold my body inside those cold bars; it locked my physical decay inside this frame.

9
00:00:55,632 --> 00:01:01,224
I am entirely free to walk the streets now, yet I look younger than my own crime.

10
00:01:01,524 --> 00:01:08,220
If the canvas took every single year of my suffering, what happens when it finally runs out of paint?
